00:00:03.830 --> 00:00:05.590 align:start position:0%
in 2013 the company i co-founded soylent  broke crowdfunding records and raised  three million dollars for a bizarre  new food product that very few people  had actually tried  new direct-to-consumer e-commerce  companies are dying a dozen nowadays  but back then there wasn't an  established playbook for how to launch a  new product  we stumbled into one of the most  effective viral marketing techniques  by complete accident and my experience  launching soylent  still informs a lot of how i think about  business today  i'm john coogan and this is how we  launched soylent  i moved to silicon valley in 2012 with  the hope of starting a traditional  technology company  something that would live on the web or  in the app store and hopefully grow  exponentially like the many successful  tech companies that had come before  but building a great tech product proved  to be harder  than i originally thought my first  company was focused on education  technology  and was essentially a quiz app to help  students memorize flashcards in a fun  and interactive way  i was still learning to program and had  no idea how to actually grow a new  product after i published it  months went by and nothing i built  gained any traction  but this was extremely common most  startups fail after all  fortunately i had been living in a sort  of hacker house  with another startup that was going  through similar challenges  everyone was working hard and basically  coding around the clock  but we kept failing to get users and  actually grow the things that we built  it was a constant cycle of come up with  an idea  build a minimum viable product launch it  and then watch our analytics flatline  it was a tough experience but we really  enjoyed the process of building new  things  so the day-to-day work was actually  quite fun after my startup ran out of  the seventeen thousand dollars we had  raised in funding  i informally joined the other startup  team who still had about a hundred  thousand dollars left in funding from y  combinator  one of paul graham's most hilariously  simple pieces of startup wisdom  is just don't die he wrote an essay  where he said  if you can just avoid dying you get rich  that sounds like a joke  but it's actually a pretty good  description of what happens in a typical  startup  so we took this to heart with no revenue  coming in we set out to cut our expenses  as much as possible  we moved to a tiny one bedroom apartment  in the tenderloin district of san  francisco  where i slept on a twin bed in the  living room we paid our rent a year in  advance  so we at least knew that we would have a  place to live  as long as we were able to pay for food  and internet service we knew we could  continue to try new ideas until  something took off  i was still focused on writing code and  building traditional software startups  but one of the roommates rob reinhart  broadened his vision and started  researching biology  he noticed that we were spending most of  our money on food  and we were eating extremely unhealthy  diets of mostly ramen noodles and costco  corn dogs  so he started working on a solution that  would be healthy convenient  and affordable typically food options  only satisfy two of these criteria  nice restaurants are healthy and  convenient but expensive  fast food is convenient and affordable  but unhealthy  and growing your own food is affordable  and healthy but is inconvenient in a  tiny apartment  the idea he came up with was simple buy  the exact nutrients needed to satisfy  the fda recommended daily values  and blend them together into a drink  that was quick and cheap to make  he started tinkering with formulas and  to be honest the first time he told me  about the project  i wrote it off entirely i simply thought  that all viable startups had to be tech  companies  and that there was no way we could  launch a new food brand  it was an interesting experiment but i  didn't see how it could make any money  but rob liked the product he had made  and thought others might too  so he started blogging about his  experience he posted a lengthy blog post  on february 13th of 2013  where he detailed his experience using  the soylent prototype formula  and shared how his health had improved  while using the product exclusively  rob has always been a creative writer  and he has a knack for crafting  interesting and provocative narratives  he called the piece how i stopped eating  food and explained that he had been  using soylent exclusively for 30 days  straight  with only positive health changes to  show for it  the blog post received mixed reviews on  hacker news with some people claiming it  was satirical  while others claimed it must be a scam  but a few people were genuinely engaging  with the ideas  and said that they would like to try the  formula as long as they could  independently vet the ingredients  so rob posted the ingredients the next  day and the idea started to grow  soon reporters from vice magazine and  discovery news  started interviewing rob to hear about  the experiment and seemingly overnight  10 000 people had signed up on an  extremely simple landing page to try the  product when it was available  with so much organic interest in the  product we were able to launch a  crowdfunding campaign  that raised three million dollars which  was a record for food products at the  time  and still stands in the consumer package  goods category after working for nearly  a year on failed idea after failed idea  we had finally lucked into something  that spoke to people and was able to  gain organic traction  we didn't realize it at the time but by  stress testing soylent on himself  by eating it exclusively for 30 days  straight rob had stumbled upon one of  the oldest marketing techniques around  david ogilvy is known as the father of  advertising and had one of the most  successful careers in advertising  throughout the 20th century  he's produced iconic advertisements for  rolls-royce  dove and countless other american  heritage brands  in 1963 ogilvy published confessions of  an advertising man  which is still remarkably relevant today  even though the mediums have shifted  from radio to podcasts  from tv to instagram and from newspapers  to tweets  all of the methods he outlines speak to  fundamental human nature  and are therefore timeless he has a  chapter in his book titled  how to make tv commercials that sell  where he outlines a variety of  techniques like humor  testimonials problem solution and  emotion  these are all still used regularly today  but he highlights demonstrations  as particularly effective he says that  demonstrations don't have to be dull  and shares one particularly interesting  commercial that his firm  created for the super glue brand loctite  in the ad super glue is applied to the  soles of an actor's shoes  before lifting him onto the ceiling  where he sticks  and hangs upside down the visual is  quite striking and it makes sure that  the actor will have the viewers full  attention while delivering the sales  pitch  the ad was so successful that it made  loctite superglue  the brand leader and the commercial won  first prize at the con festival  this ad has even been remade by loctite  and directly copied by  several competitors over the past few  decades  the strategy of a shocking demonstration  has also been used to sell other  products like dog food  back in the 1980s alpo dog food had  hired the actor lorne greene  as a spokesperson and in each commercial  they were careful to point out that  lorne  indeed fed alpo to his dogs it's also a  bit of a legend that the president of  call con pet food  would personally eat a can of his  company's dog food  at the annual shareholders meeting each  year but one can of dog food isn't  nearly as impressive as a full month of  dog food  so naturally mitch felderhoff the  president of the munster milling company  ate nothing but munster brand dog food  for 30 days at the start of 2020  the marketing stunt worked for munster  milling and the press covered mitch's  dog food diet extensively  these extreme demonstrations are good  for two reasons  first they let the customer know that  you have faith in your product  and are willing to put your own body on  the line to test it  before products have independent reviews  customer testimonials  or slick ads sometimes the only advocate  for the product  is the founder the second benefit of a  stress test  is that it is extremely hard to ignore  some people love these stunts  some people hate them but very few  people can disregard them entirely  everyone had an opinion on soylent when  we launched it which meant lots of  attention  the press wanted to hear the full story  and people who read stories about  soylent  couldn't resist commenting on how crazy  this silicon valley guy was  for giving up food entirely for 30 days  straight  now is a stress test demonstration right  for every product launch  absolutely not it's only appropriate  when your product is significantly  different from the competition  and would naturally draw a lot of  skepticism from potential customers  but that doesn't mean that it's not  important to focus on telling a  compelling story  when you launch the product there are  plenty of founders who lament the fact  that no journalist wants to write  about what they are building but  journalists only write about things that  people will read  they need to find compelling stories  good stories have clear narrative arcs  for soylent it was a broke entrepreneur  struggling to find a way to stay healthy  who took a big risk by testing a new  product on himself for 30 days straight  it's a dramatic story like someone  walking a tightrope between two  buildings  will they fall will rob's soylent  experiment wind up having some  unforeseen health consequences  it's human nature to want to find out  what happens  so that's how we launched soylent we  focused on making a product that solved  a real problem we had  ourselves and then we told the world  about it in the most dramatic way  possible  none of us had studied marketing or even  heard of david ogilvy before  but we knew what made for an interesting  experiment and that sharing detailed  information would spark people's  interest  hopefully you enjoyed this video i'm  planning to post a few more about my  personal entrepreneurial experiences  so please subscribe to the channel if  you haven't already if you'd like to get  in touch with me  just reach out on twitter and as always  thanks for watching
 

